## 8. Wave-18 developer loop amendment (DX4)

Implementer: Codex for Charlie Cheever, 2026-10-19, wave-18 fixes #3/#4/#8.
SEC4 owns security campaigns and lint semantics; QRY owns read semantics.
MIG's staging clock is governed by LLP 3000.000.000.002 §4 and unchanged.

`seedAccounts` accepts `service` (default operator, same 1–64 character seat
validation as operatorToken) and merges `args(account)` over `{principal, role}`.
Explicit custom keys override defaults. It validates the seat before effects;
production/dev refusal still precedes mint/signup. Only the chosen service's
existing mutation authority applies. First-admin's tested guide authenticates
first, tries signup only after failed login, and calls the operator-only roster
upsert. Repetition preserves the principal and role; wrong credentials cannot
replace an account or grant. Bulk batches still create fresh random accounts.

Proof `startDev` echoes the closed-door guidance to caller stderr after matching
its per-launch /auth/config marker. Guides start/cheatsheet/personas surface
staging in the ordinary loop. No proof success is inferred from the notice.

Disposable dev already uses a private file/WAL store. It now retains an Owner
and publishes the identical mutual-HMAC/AEAD local token proof as persistent
dev. Locked, private project registry entries include an optional `storePath`.
`token --service` selects a unique live project entry; `--port N` selects among
entries (including closed-door password-only hosts). `--release` is exclusive
with --port. The selected path must still pass existing hardened auxiliary-file,
store-identity, live-owner and mutual-proof checks. Registry data alone grants no
authority; no bearer can mint and no unprotected token door is added. Multiple
hosts refuse implicit selection. Explicit unmatched ports refuse without
falling back to another store. Older records without a proof path request a
restart. Live minting uses the current host clock, matching session resolution
and staging, so time-travel dev does not mint already-expired tokens. Offline
minting retains wall time. Disposable directory ownership and cleanup last for
the whole host lifetime.

Session lifetimes stay seven days on the authoritative application clock.
Every successful or post-advance maintenance-failed clock reply includes a
sessionNotice explaining login after large jumps and upload after time travel;
the clock CLI and staging proof helper echo it on stderr. Reset replies state
that accounts/sessions were removed and cached credentials must be discarded,
then users recreated and roles reprovisioned. This deliberately preserves auth
expiry testing rather than silently extending credentials. Production keeps
its generic auth refusals. Staging's real throttles stay unchanged; the refusal
names Retry-After/retryAfterMs and seedAccounts. Dev loopback already has no
minute counters: scripted dev must serialize KDF work rather than raise a
nonexistent minute limit. This choice avoids a staging/production auth fork.

Upgrade scans bounded authored JS/TS/shell/JSON sources for /seed, /dev/*,
persona headers and startDev calls, skips dependencies/generated/store/output
folders and symlinks, prints per-project paths and migration actions even for
quiet/JSON callers, and returns warnings in JSON. A bounded/incomplete scan
says so. It reports candidates, not a claim that every listed startDev call
lacks --memory. Password-only doors closed and helper persistence began at
0.4.33; scanning also covers surviving risky scripts upgrading from 0.4.33.
Store-lock errors retain recorded PID/address and recommend disposable memory
proofs or stopping the recorded owner. Port conflict probes consult public
/auth/config when closed dev /dev/status refuses; they do not infer non-dev
from a 403.

Evidence: affected unit/native/client tests, packed guide recipes and DX4
cases in Linux consumer qualification; copied 5×wave18 and 5×wave17 upgrade
warning transcripts, assigned Mac Studio affected tests, full crate suites,
ref/docs budget and repository inventory/guide checks. Actual results and any
limits are in WORKSTREAM-REPORT.md; no publication claim or version bump.

