llp/3000.000.000-retained-storage-and-hot-release.rfc.md:1:# LLP 3000.000.000: Retained storage and hot release activation
llp/3000.000.000-retained-storage-and-hot-release.rfc.md:16:## 1. Commission and scope
llp/3000.000.000-retained-storage-and-hot-release.rfc.md:18:Charlie approved the ideal end-game release lifecycle and commissioned implementation on 2026-10-12. Implementer: this ROLL session, beginning that date. Wave 8 DT1 found additive rollback deletes new storage in all 50 day-two lanes; DT1a found unnamed enum conflicts; DT10 found deployment requires stopping serve. This supersedes the release-only removal semantics in LLP 3000.000's implementation follow-up. Development and device replacement keep their existing projection/reset semantics. No new publication authority or gate is introduced.
llp/3000.000.000-retained-storage-and-hot-release.rfc.md:20:## 2. Retain by default
llp/3000.000.000-retained-storage-and-hot-release.rfc.md:22:Every release activation (deploy and rollback) retains removed durable application tables, columns and index definitions in SQLite-owned dormant storage within the same activation transaction. The active r/i image remains an exact projection of the active schema. Dormant data is outside that namespace and unavailable to programs, rules, references, queries, sync, SQL application projections and clients. The Quarry/IR binder admits only declared application table names; it has no general application SQL evaluator. Dormant internal names are not added to Schema, and schema validation forbids declarations in the reserved `_dormant_` and `_release_` namespaces. Inspection `snapback4 sql` is owner-only and may enumerate it. A future application SQL adapter must keep its declared authorized input allow-list. Asset reads must resolve the home table, column and row in the active schema/r image and check the current read rule; a dormant home refuses E_ASSET_UNKNOWN even when a caller knows its id. Test the reserved-name/program boundary and direct fetch by id. Reintroducing a dormant name deliberately reactivates its data, after full validation under the candidate schema. A deployment that reuses any dormant name refuses unless --restore-dormant explicitly selects restoration; its refusal lists retained items/counts/samples and the alternatives `deploy <dir> --restore-dormant` or `prune <dir> --allow-destructive` before fresh creation. Rollback's selection of an immutable older release explicitly selects restoration; `rollback <dir> --to N` also selects an immutable release for deliberate roll forward. To convert durable to ephemeral, deploy a release without that table, prune it explicitly, then deploy the ephemeral definition. This applies to deployments as well as rollback; a rename remains two names, without an inferred data move.
llp/3000.000.000-retained-storage-and-hot-release.rfc.md:24:Inventory and audit entries carry a stable typed key (`table:name`, `column:table.name`, or `index:table.name`); pagination uses the returned key, not a bare path that could collide between columns and indexes. The SQLite format is `_dormant_tables(table_name, definition)` for whole-table Table definitions, `_dormant_rows(t,id,data)` for their stored images, `_dormant_columns(table_name,column_name,definition)` for retired column type/default/ownership, `_dormant_values(t,id,column_name,data)` for their cells, `_dormant_indexes(table_name,index_name,definition)` for index definitions and `_dormant_refs(t,id,column_name,parent_t,parent_id,invalid)` for deletion evidence with a parent lookup index. Catalog numbers are reserved monotonically and reused for the same physical name, but dormant rows are never kept in r under a hidden catalog number. These tables are created only during successful release activation; legacy deployments start empty and cannot recover data previously deleted by older binaries. A successful release activation writes the release-only format marker `version=snapback4-release-store-3`. New Store open/version checks accept this, the prior snapback4-release-store-2 and the legacy snapback4-store-1; old binaries reject it with E_STORE rather than opening it. Development and device stores retain snapback4-store-1 and their formats are unchanged. The format marker rolls back with failed activation. On restore, omitted cells from whole-table images move to dormant column cells before projection, merging previous retired cells; full-table entries are consumed only on commit.
llp/3000.000.000-retained-storage-and-hot-release.rfc.md:44:Partial restoration consumes only candidate-visible cells: B's T(a,b) → absent T → C's T(a) restores a and moves b to dormant cells, with b's definition, then B restores b. For an identical (t,id,column) cell key, the newly retired active value wins: it is the most recent authoritative value. Preserve other dormant keys. Use transactional upsert and test retire → restore → update → retire → restore. Keep zero-row table/column/index definitions too. Whole-table restoration first restores its stored image, splits omitted cells into dormancy, then projects the active image. Consumption shares commit with validation. Refuse application-table to derived-table ownership changes, or authoritative-column to maintained/captured ownership changes, including when the application table/column was dormant. Derived caches never overwrite retained authoritative data. The refusal names the incompatible owner and directs a deliberate rename or prune while dormant.
llp/3000.000.000-retained-storage-and-hot-release.rfc.md:46:At retirement record reference edges for dormant rows/cells (including optional/list references), bound to their current parent lifetime. An active delete transaction marks matching dormant edges invalid before the id can be reused, even for identical replacement values. Only a move into dormant storage or replacement of a derived cache is exempt from deletion invalidation. Actual authoritative-row deletion always invalidates matching dormant edges, including expiry during activation; failed activation rolls the invalidation back. A fixed-size identity migration rewrites dormant whole rows, cells, narrowed rows and their edge ledger when the referenced derived target lifetime survives migration; removing that target retires its lifetime, so its dormant edges remain bound to the retired address and restoration refuses rather than attaching them to a later reintroduction. Restoration refuses invalidated edges or missing parents with table/column/parent ids, counts and samples; it cannot silently recapture another parent's audience. For read-through rows, compare preserved captured audience and identity fields to the candidate parent before existing recapture work. A changed audience refuses restoration; inherited deadlines keep their original capture. Newly expired rows remain logically invisible and may be swept only under the reactivated schema. Test deletion/reuse, identical replacement, changed audience, nested references and expiry. This adds no device incarnation format.
llp/3000.000.000-retained-storage-and-hot-release.rfc.md:50:## 3. Older writes and incompatible values
llp/3000.000.000-retained-storage-and-hot-release.rfc.md:52:Updating an active row while its newer columns are dormant preserves those dormant values. Deleting that row deletes its dormant column values in the same transaction: forward never resurrects a deleted row, including when its id is reused. New rows written under the older schema have no dormant value. On forward they receive the candidate literal default or optional null; required columns without such a default refuse with exact missing-row counts and up to five ids. Repair by deploying a bridge schema with that column optional or a literal default using --restore-dormant, fill it through a bridge mutation, then deploy the desired required schema. Alternatively explicitly delete the named rows through the current release and retry. Never tell the older schema to write a field it lacks. Contextual now/viewer defaults do not invent historical values. Existing required-column backfill rules still apply.
llp/3000.000.000-retained-storage-and-hot-release.rfc.md:58:## 4. Explicit pruning and audit
llp/3000.000.000-retained-storage-and-hot-release.rfc.md:60:snapback4 prune <dir> runs through the serving owner when live, otherwise the offline owner. It lists dormant tables, columns and indexes with row counts, logical serialized dormant bytes (including retained asset bytes separately) and at most five ids; it changes nothing without --allow-destructive. With that flag it permanently deletes exactly the currently dormant storage in one writer transaction, records a durable prune event, and leaves active rows/programs untouched. It releases dormant asset homes for the ordinary collector. Deploy/rollback --allow-destructive do not prune; direct users to the separate command. Prune intentionally deletes all dormant items as one reviewed storage boundary; per-item dependency-aware pruning is deferred because partial pruning can strand dormant references and asset homes. There is no implicit size or age collector: previews make storage growth visible without inventing a data-loss policy. After prune, reintroducing the schema gets empty tables and normal required-column/default behavior. Pruning is an irreversible operator action; the command output names every item dropped.
llp/3000.000.000-retained-storage-and-hot-release.rfc.md:66:## 5. Hot activation and ownership
llp/3000.000.000-retained-storage-and-hot-release.rfc.md:68:serve owns the database sidecar for its lifetime and publishes a Unix-domain release-control socket in the private per-user runtime directory (§5.1; directory 0700, socket 0600). Check peer credentials on both ends (Linux SO_PEERCRED, Darwin getpeereid), requiring the effective UID. Only the host filesystem owner has this capability; it is not an HTTP door, browser origin, bearer service seat or remote endpoint. Check filesystem objects before replacing a stale socket; never follow symlinks or replace a regular file. Use `.release-command` (a hardened private regular file, no-follow, single link) for the command flock and the deterministic runtime address (§5.1) for the control socket. lstat socket entries; only a same-owner socket may be removed while holding the exclusive store sidecar. Bound command JSON to 4096 bytes and response JSON to 64 KiB, with a 5-second input timeout and 120-second command response timeout. The trusted same-UID operator can directly edit source/database; malicious same-UID edits are outside the threat model, but ordinary source races are caught by a candidate content digest over source/native/worker files in the request and verified by the server. Numeric release id is the only source selector; never a caller path. Commands hold a separate private command flock while allocating/staging immutable release source. They acquire the store owner when stopped; E_STORE_BUSY with a running serve uses the local socket instead of opening another writer. Dev and an older/unreachable server continue to refuse with an exact stop/retry command.
llp/3000.000.000-retained-storage-and-hot-release.rfc.md:70:snapback4 releases <dir> reads authoritative current release, activation revision, generation and durable events without adopting source, including while serve is live. This is the recovery command for an ambiguous result. A bounded command message identifies the owned database inode/canonical identity, candidate release id, expected current release and monotone activation revision, operation and explicit flags. The server compiles only its own immutable release directory, prepares native module/worker prerequisites before commit, then holds its existing engine writer guard for activation and in-memory publication. The server verifies both expected current release and activation revision inside the activation transaction. Increment activation revision on every successful release activation, including an equal-hash source change; A→B→A cannot satisfy an old command. Concurrent commands serialize on the command flock; out-of-band stale commands fail compare-and-swap. No request may choose arbitrary filesystem source. Compilation/validation failure leaves the previous engine, bound schema, native module, worker revision and reader factory unchanged.
llp/3000.000.000-retained-storage-and-hot-release.rfc.md:72:A query already running in a pinned SQLite read transaction finishes on its old snapshot and reports that generation. A writer already executing finishes before activation obtains its engine guard. Queued HTTP operations are admitted under one release request guard covering signature lookup, argument decoding and execution. Activation acquires its exclusive side before taking the engine guard; admitted operations finish before activation, and later operations dispatch under the new generation. Internal detached readers still fence obsolete identities with E_GENERATION. Fresh requests after publication use the new engine; a stale pooled reader not yet pinned retries through the replacement factory. Prepare a runtime bundle containing bound programs, precompiled native module/metadata, worker root and validated named declarations; prepare the reader factory from the post-activation engine while its writer guard blocks new writers (reader opening failure after commit clears the old factory and uses existing lazy retry, never aborts a committed activation or advertises the old backend). Swap bound/generation/native under the common bound guard in the existing /schema guard order, retire pooled readers, rebind ephemeral state, invalidate change answers, wake sync/polls and update worker revision while holding the writer guard. The supervisor explicitly switches its immutable release project root and named declarations on revision, including addition/removal/replacement; externally supplied --worker stays an operator-owned entry. Worker preparation captures an immutable (root, entry, declarations/environment, supervisor revision) tuple. Under the engine guard it compares that captured revision to the current worker revision before minting credentials; stale preparation is discarded and rediscovered. Never relabel an old prepared source with the latest revision. Credentials bind that checked revision so later activation fences them even when spawn finishes later. Workers restart using the candidate packaged source and native continuation hashes keep the existing conservative parking policy. Long validation can pause writes; hot means no process restart, not an unbounded zero-pause latency promise.
llp/3000.000.000-retained-storage-and-hot-release.rfc.md:74:The server continues an admitted activation even if the client hits its 120-second response timeout or closes the socket; failure to write the reply is not permission to roll back or exit the server. The database is authoritative at crash or lost socket reply. Timeout/EOF after sending a command reports an ambiguous result and instructs inspection of current and durable history; never silently resend as a new release or claim failure left the old release active. Before activation fsync required immutable source/native/worker files and their directories, then the published releases directory entry. The supported guarantee covers process crashes and filesystem/SQLite durability on hosts honoring fsync; no guarantee covers failing storage hardware. Restart attaches the database-selected release even if current's convenience copy is stale. After database commit, publication is infallible in-memory assignments and committed ephemeral rebind; fallible native compilation, declaration/environment/import validation happens before commit. Worker process spawn remains fallible after commit: failure reports committed activation with degraded worker availability and retries; it cannot undo a release or external effects. Filesystem-copy failures report the committed result.
llp/3000.000.000-retained-storage-and-hot-release.rfc.md:76:### 5.1 Path-independent control address
llp/3000.000.000-retained-storage-and-hot-release.rfc.md:81:let the existing total input deadline govern clients that write after accept.
llp/3000.000.000-retained-storage-and-hot-release.rfc.md:97:its canonical directory once, then use that directory for ownership and all
llp/3000.000.000-retained-storage-and-hot-release.rfc.md:110:A moved deployment must stop and restart serve, as moving its immutable source
llp/3000.000.000-retained-storage-and-hot-release.rfc.md:122:Bind only while holding the exclusive database sidecar. An existing entry
llp/3000.000.000-retained-storage-and-hot-release.rfc.md:123:must be a same-owner 0600 socket. Probe it: a live listener refuses without
llp/3000.000.000-retained-storage-and-hot-release.rfc.md:126:peer checks on both ends, envelope identity, command flock and revision CAS.
llp/3000.000.000-retained-storage-and-hot-release.rfc.md:128:nonblocking setup; Drop removes only that owned socket inode. Graceful shutdown
llp/3000.000.000-retained-storage-and-hot-release.rfc.md:129:joins control before releasing store ownership. Fatal exit after permission setup leaves a stale
llp/3000.000.000-retained-storage-and-hot-release.rfc.md:130:socket for the next exclusive owner. A crash between bind and chmod may leave
llp/3000.000.000-retained-storage-and-hot-release.rfc.md:143:while Shared still retains the owner. An admitted activation is allowed to
llp/3000.000.000-retained-storage-and-hot-release.rfc.md:144:finish. Enforce the five-second input bound as a total monotonic deadline,
llp/3000.000.000-retained-storage-and-hot-release.rfc.md:146:arriving repeatedly cannot reset the deadline or prolong shutdown. The signal
llp/3000.000.000-retained-storage-and-hot-release.rfc.md:150:## 6. Devices and partitions
llp/3000.000.000-retained-storage-and-hot-release.rfc.md:154:Lock order: command flock is CLI-only; serving control never acquires it. Candidate filesystem/native/worker preparation precedes engine lock. Serving publication holds engine → bound → generation → native, and separately replaces readers while engine is held (matching existing checkout); no /schema handler takes engine while holding bound. Ephemeral rebind uses its existing committed infallible path, never a fallible post-commit store read. Update desired worker root before revision; worker thread releases root/bound guards before taking engine. Wake/seq/answers follow existing publish_engine while engine is held: these are short memory updates/nonblocking condition notifications, required before new writer admission; no socket/child wait occurs there. `releases` includes the existing worker status metadata so post-commit launch failure is visible as degraded availability. No SQLite lock is held while waiting for a child process or socket peer.
llp/3000.000.000-retained-storage-and-hot-release.rfc.md:156:## 7. Implementation and acceptance
llp/3000.000.000-retained-storage-and-hot-release.rfc.md:160:CLI matrix: `deploy <dir> [--restore-dormant] [--allow-feed-adoption]`; `rollback <dir> [--to N] [--allow-feed-adoption]`; `prune <dir> [--allow-destructive]`; `releases <dir>`. Legacy --allow-destructive on deploy/rollback is accepted but does not prune or bypass validation, and prints the prune command when relevant. --restore-dormant authorizes only restoration, never loss. --to requires a positive existing immutable release id and may select a newer release. E_ADOPT operator text: `table.column/index: N affected rows; sample ids [...]; values/keys [...]; reason; rewrite: <bridge/repair schema or command>; inspect: snapback4 sql <query> --release <dir>`. No flag bypasses invalid values, ownership or references. Unreachable live control refuses `E_STORE_BUSY: stop snapback4 serve <dir>, rerun the exact command, then snapback4 serve <dir>` before send; after send use `releases <dir>` and explicit `rollback --to N` after inspecting the outcome, never an automatic deploy allocating N+1.
llp/3000.000.000-retained-storage-and-hot-release.rfc.md:162:History/status is page-based with `releases <dir> [--after <event-id>]`; bounded pages return a continuation and inventory pages use stable item keys. Prune audit stores a complete item list in SQLite; bounded replies carry a continuation rather than truncate silently. Critical assertions include T(a,b) → absent T → T(a) → absent T → T(a,b), preserved b and latest updated a; identical parent id/value replacement invalidates dormant edges; stale revision after A→B→A and equal-hash activation refuses; activation-time parent expiry followed by identical-id replacement invalidates dormant references; rapid B→C activation with a barrier between B worker preparation and credential minting discards B conventional/named source; an old checked-out reader not yet pinned retries the new factory including native-only changes; a connected client holding native module A hot-adopts B with unchanged declarations, reacquires B backend/module and refuses a delayed A result; inject interruption before/after commit and during pointer-copy updates to distinguish no activation from committed activation with an ambiguous result.
llp/3000.000.000-retained-storage-and-hot-release.rfc.md:166:## 8. Review disposition and continuation
llp/3000.000.000-retained-storage-and-hot-release.rfc.md:168:Four initial revisions/review rounds are recorded verbatim under llp/reviews/. Muse returned READY on revision 4. Codex identified actual-deletion evidence, revision-bound worker preparation, and native-only content in semantic generation identity; all are explicit above. The coordinator subsequently authorized targeted confirmation, further review rounds and judgment calls, and commissioned implementation through tests and commits without another approval stop.
llp/3000.000.000-retained-storage-and-hot-release.rfc.md:172:## 9. Implementation record
llp/3000.000.000-retained-storage-and-hot-release.rfc.md:174:The core implements dormant tables/cells/index definitions and reference-lifetime evidence, partial restoration, required-value/enum/unique/cap refusal populations, prune/history, release-only format fencing, revision CAS and native digest in activation_hash. Nineteen focused retention regressions pass, including activation-time expiry, assets, typed audit pagination and ownership refusal.
llp/3000.000.000-retained-storage-and-hot-release.rfc.md:176:The host implements the private same-owner transport, prepared native/worker custody, atomic live engine publication, reader fencing, revision-bound worker preparation, named-worker mutation and database-authoritative crash recovery. Seven focused hot-activation tests and four transport tests pass; the complete host unit suite passes (393 tests plus four existing ignored). All 23 release CLI/client integration tests pass, including an already-connected native-only client, concurrent traffic, hot narrowing refusal and process death before/after commit. Restoring full tables installs the identity scan before read-through adoption. Historical recorded attribution (including nulls), parent bindings and captured deadlines survive restoration; only physically missing captures are filled, and effective deadlines recompute under the candidate. An older release may make a through reference mutable; restoration refuses if its current parent table/id differs from the retained binding. Both naming orders of joint parent/child retirement, an unswept expired attribution target, effective deadline visibility, partial capture deletion/reuse and older retargeting are regressions. A test-only publication barrier permits an owned subprocess kill after commit and before runtime/pointer/reply publication, followed by database-authoritative recovery and retained forward restoration. Named secret selections are validated in immutable preparation before commit. The device and simulator exercise delayed old snapshots and audiences on both stores. SERVER.md owns the lifecycle semantics, the evolution card supplies the schema bridge, and the OPS deploy card is a one-line pointer. Final full quality results and commits are recorded in WORKSTREAM-REPORT.md. No publication is claimed.
llp/3000.000.000-retained-storage-and-hot-release.rfc.md:181:## 8. Integration with the loop (2026-10-12)
llp/3000.000.000-retained-storage-and-hot-release.rfc.md:209:the first owner's socket. Its E_STORE_BUSY retains the canonical owner address
llp/3000.000.000-retained-storage-and-hot-release.rfc.md:210:(or address-pending CLI holder), with the stop/retry remedy. Token forwarding
llp/3000.000.000-retained-storage-and-hot-release.rfc.md:211:continues to use the canonical database owner. Both serving aliases are tested.
llp/3000.000.000-retained-storage-and-hot-release.rfc.md:214:### Control socket portability qualification (2026-10-12)
llp/3000.000.000-retained-storage-and-hot-release.rfc.md:217:bb3f4f0ac4 (ownership-scoped control join) and 0d5d042e57 (total input
llp/3000.000.000-retained-storage-and-hot-release.rfc.md:218:deadline and exact socket permissions). Fresh Codex and Muse final reviews
llp/3000.000.000-retained-storage-and-hot-release.rfc.md:221:earlier Codex shutdown findings were reproduced and corrected, not waived.
llp/3000.000.000-retained-storage-and-hot-release.rfc.md:241:## 9. Wave 9 release lifecycle amendment
llp/3000.000.000-retained-storage-and-hot-release.rfc.md:245:### 9.1 Per-row narrowed-value dormancy
llp/3000.000.000-retained-storage-and-hot-release.rfc.md:251:At one pinned activation time, retire affected expired predecessor read-through rows under their old deadlines before narrowed hiding; retirement uses the ordinary authoritative delete to invalidate dependent evidence and remove dormant cells. Then restore candidate-compatible images, hide the incompatible/reference-dependent population, rebuild all physical r/i and _cap_counts/maintained caches, and validate before publication. Counts, sums and private parent-column mirrors all use this order. Reference closure removes at least one active row per iteration, terminates on the finite store even with nominal cycles, and any non-null element of a nested/list reference can hide its row. Null has no edge; a missing ordinary nominal target alone does not trigger hiding, while an absent target schema or narrowed-dormant target does. Capture dormant reference lifetime evidence before hiding; hiding is not deletion. Actual parent deletion invalidates evidence. Transferring a retained whole row or cell into narrowed dormancy preserves its existing bound/unbound/deleted evidence; it must never recapture a replacement parent. On candidate restoration, evidence invalidated by an actual deletion keeps a row dormant only if its reference/capture column is candidate-visible. An initially dangling nominal keep reference captures invalid=2 (unbound), distinct from invalid=1 (deleted captured lifetime); it may restore without inventing a lifetime. Deletion invalidates only bound (invalid=0) edges. Omitted reference cells and evidence stay retained for future restoration. A narrowed row never rebinds a deleted visible parent. Restored read-through images retain their historical captures and must pass the existing audience/deadline safety check. Whole-table restoration keeps the full source image through lifetime retirement and narrowed classification; ordinary candidate projection follows that boundary. Before projecting a narrowed image, evaluate retirement against its retained source definition/deadline, including direct expiry without read-through; removing read-through or expiry cannot revive an expired source lifetime. Actual retirement deletes the narrowed image, dependent captured evidence and its retained asset homes. Rows whose target table is absent stay dormant. Dormant rows run no jobs/expiry until reactivation. Assets remain retained but inaccessible. `prune --allow-destructive` drops narrowed images, associated dormant cells and lifetime evidence and releases only their asset homes; the preview names counts/bytes/sample ids. Inventory uses `rows:<table>` typed keys and audit items record before_rows, after_rows, restored_rows and bounded before_sample_ids in the activation transaction, including partial column/row restorations whose typed key remains. Per-table narrowed restoration counters distinguish restore-and-hide swaps with equal totals; summary restored_items includes partial restorations. Restoration identity sets are held transactionally and counted against final surviving r only after all read-through/cache activation phases. Column restored counts are recorded from cells actually consumed into surviving candidate rows, not inferred from inventory decreases; expiry/deletion may remove cells without restoring them. There is no automatic collection.
llp/3000.000.000-retained-storage-and-hot-release.rfc.md:253:### 9.2 Exact preview and operator vocabulary
llp/3000.000.000-retained-storage-and-hot-release.rfc.md:255:`rollback <dir> --to N --check` and `check --store <path> --release N` execute candidate adoption on a private SQLite snapshot created by VACUUM INTO through a read-only source connection. This is disk-backed, uses the exact adoption builder/validation, and never changes the live source, pointer, events or generation. SQLite supports VACUUM INTO on a read-only source; it acquires a coherent WAL read snapshot without the source writer lock. BUSY/disk failures refuse with retry advice, never fall back to copying sidecars. The temporary directory is owner-only and removed on success, refusal and ordinary unwinding. Process kill/crash may leave a private preview directory; operators can remove snapback4-release-preview-* leftovers when no preview uses them. The live format, including format 2, is never upgraded by preview. The snapshot may require disk/time proportional to the store; it provides a point-in-time plan, not a write freeze. The output enumerates the full candidate inventory and totals every narrowed population, not just the first sixteen status items; it includes any E_ADOPT refusal. Preview prepares the candidate native content identity but never starts workers or effects.
llp/3000.000.000-retained-storage-and-hot-release.rfc.md:259:### 9.3 HTTP admission and reporting
llp/3000.000.000-retained-storage-and-hot-release.rfc.md:268:### 9.4 Round-one review dispositions
llp/3000.000.000-retained-storage-and-hot-release.rfc.md:286:Round seven: Muse READY; GPT found expired narrowed images losing source deadlines when read-through is removed, initially dangling keep references misclassified during original whole-table capture, and action replies using intake generation. Fixed source-image retirement before projection, unbound classification at the original capture boundary, and action admission/collection stamps; regression coverage added. Audit counts and adoption receipts now use final surviving restoration identities after every activation phase.
llp/3000.000.000-retained-storage-and-hot-release.rfc.md:288:Round nine: Muse READY; GPT found premature whole-table projection losing source deadlines, the source-image predicate omitting direct-expiry removal, and missing query arguments using the runtime 200 status. Preserved whole source images through classification, retired restored source lifetimes before hiding/projection (direct and inherited), and normalized query input refusals to the specified 400 envelope. New regressions cover expired and live full-table transfers and missing/non-object query args.
llp/3000.000.000-retained-storage-and-hot-release.rfc.md:290:Round ten: Muse READY; GPT found asset byte/kind/MIME incompatibilities bypassing dormancy and later refusing activation. Classification now shares the existing ledger-backed asset validator for scalar/optional/list leaves on hiding and restoration. Author also verified a legal table named values broke unquoted inspection SQL; every hint now quotes the table identifier, with a live SQL regression. MEDIA retains upload/outbox ownership; this is release adoption only.
