llp/3000.009-general-purpose-backend.rfc.md:1478:### Acceptance, review and remaining choices
# Rules

**Snapback 2 optimizes the edit-to-verified loop — the agent's building an app on it, and ours building it.**

Speed is the constraint. Everything below exists to keep the edit-to-verified loop short
and to stop this repository accreting apparatus faster than anyone can remove it. These
rules bind `snapback2/` and the LLPs numbered 1000 and up; Snapback 1 is baselined, not
exempt, and its numbers may only fall.

Each rule is **[check]** (enforced by `caps`) or **[review]** (enforced by a human).

## Budgets — every budget is a trade, not a limit

- **5 blocking checks, 60s total.** A sixth requires deleting one, in the same PR. **[check]**
- **59 documents in the working set** (`llp/current/`). Today's count. A 60th requires
  archiving one — which is just removing the link. Snapback 2 aims to leave this at 15;
  Snapback 1's links are archived at promotion. **[check]**
- **12 documents in the foundation** (`llp/foundation/`). **[check]**
- **This file: 700 words.** If it grows, something becomes a check or stops being a rule. **[check]**
- **1,500 lines per source file, baseline 204.** Generated files exempt — and generated
  files are built, not committed; so are benchmark run records under `benchmarks/runs/`,
  which are evidence, not source. The baseline is Snapback 1's oversize count on the day
  the rule landed; it may not grow, and `snapback2/` contributes zero to it. **[check]**

## Blocking checks

`caps` · `ref-check` · `snapback2/scripts/test.sh` · `snapback2/scripts/measure.mjs --check` · `check-inventories`

## Loop shape

- **Every check reports all failures in one run.** Fail-fast is banned. **[check]**
- **Nothing blocks on anything slower than 60 seconds.** Slow verification runs on a
  schedule, attributed to the breaking commit, and files a bounded set of issues. **[review]**
- **Fix loops get 3 rounds.** Then stop: escalate to a human or descope. **[review]**

## Time budgets

Tracked every run of `snapback2/scripts/measure.mjs` (appends `snapback2/metrics/loop.jsonl`).
A regression is a P0 with a name on it.

| | budget |
|---|---|
| Incremental rebuild after a one-line edit | 15s |
| Test what you just changed | 30s |
| Blocking gate suite | 60s |
| Full build, warm cache | 5 min |
| **`snapback2 check` on a program (the agent's product latency)** | **1s** |
| **`snapback2 doctor` boot-to-ready** | **0.5s** |

## Scope

- **`NOT-DOING.md` is binding.** Moving something onto the doing-list means writing why
  and taking something off. **[review]**
- **Delete; don't deprecate.** No compat shims or migration paths before promotion. **[review]**
- **Nothing is compiled, transpiled, or generated at runtime that could be built ahead of
  time.** **[review]**
- **A spec needs an implementer and a date, or it isn't written.** **[review]**
- **Only implemented specs bind.** A document with no running code behind it is a
  proposal, and a proposal cannot block a PR or justify a check. **[review]**

## Agents

- **Agents remove apparatus freely and add none.** An agent PR that adds a check, script,
  registry, config file, or design doc needs explicit human approval saying so. **[review]**
- **2026-09-06 session exception:** the user delegated the search implementation
  decisions; one `snapback2-core/build.rs` may generate pinned Unicode tables at
  build time, alongside the existing package build. No new gate, registry,
  runtime generator, or Unicode dependency is authorized by this exception. **[review]**
- **Snapback 2 LLPs are numbered 1000 and up**; `llp-create` must be told. **[review]**
- **Snapback 4 LLPs are numbered 3000 and up** (`snapback4/`, LLP 3000); it adopts these budgets
  and loop shape as its own and keeps its scope in `snapback4/NOT-DOING.md`. **[review]**

## Shared machines

- Warm, named lanes, one per stream of work. Never clone fresh; reuse the worktree.
- **Never `git stash`.** Use a temporary commit.
- Kill only PIDs you recorded at launch. Never by name or pattern.
- **Second candidate, `ccb9f752d1`:** this commit added the test-only wait. Native
  and the Expo witness passed on Xcode 27.1. Linux attempt 2 hit ENOSPC on the
  shared disk; attempt 3 passed with `== 0 failure(s) in 3567 s`.
- **Mac dry run 3 at `ccb9f752d1`:** `cargo test` was green, but the run was
  invalidated by a clamshell sleep on battery mid-run (23:10Z–01:57Z), which
  stretched `ordinary-search-oracle.test.mjs` and failed it. Run alone on AC with
  the lid open, that file passed 8/8.

The 0.4.33 Mac gate now watches AC power, the lid and sleep during the run, and
restarts an attempt it marks environment-invalid. Details:
[reports/20261017-publication-0.4.31/REPORT.md](reports/20261017-publication-0.4.31/REPORT.md).

Integration 18 upgrade correction: retained executable identity ignores the
compiler-generated device compatibility report, whose server version changes
on a CLI upgrade. The full control-request digest still covers that report;
activation recompiles/rechecks the unchanged retained source. An actual 0.4.33
store with declared privacy intent and rollback history exposed this mismatch.
The focused unit regression distinguishes identity from integrity and preserves
source/native/worker byte changes as new executable content. Proof hosts also
honor their explicit binary environment so qualification cannot accidentally
select a stale checkout release binary.

## 2026-10-19 — DX4: find staging before opening dev doors

Wave-18 fixes #3/#4/#7/#8 put real-user staging in the default start, cheatsheet
and personas loop. Password-only proof hosts echo closed-door guidance to the
caller. Role provisioning accepts a service seat and merges custom arguments;
first-admin is replayable. Per-project upgrades identify closed-door scripts and
persistent startDev calls. Store ownership, clock/reset and identity refusals
explain the recovery action. Memory dev hosts support the same private mutual
proof as persistent hosts; live service tokens use the host clock. No public
fixture/token door or weaker staging identity policy was added.

Uploads now point document callers at explicit file verification and explain
uploader ownership, collection and one home. The complete installed guide
recipe exercises a PDF, refusals, references and downloads. The packed DX4 hook
also checks default guide output, custom provisioning, memory tokens, clock
recovery and upgrade scans. The new recipe's mutations are online-only; its
authoring census adds two `never` entries and no predicted private write sites.

LLP 3000.000.000.001 §8 and LLP 3000.038 §12 preserve the decisions. Muse and
a fresh Codex session both returned READY in round 2; verbatim evidence is in
`llp/reviews/dx4/`. MIG owns the staging clock, SEC4 the security checks and
QRY read semantics. Final qualification, copied-app warning transcripts and
limits are recorded in the root WORKSTREAM-REPORT.md. No bump or push.

exec
/bin/bash -lc "sed -n '60,115p' llp/3000.000.000-retained-storage-and-hot-release.rfc.md && sed -n '130,165p' llp/3000.000.000-retained-storage-and-hot-release.rfc.md && sed -n '1,70p' llp/3000.011.003.000.000-packaged-proof-kit.rfc.md && sed -n '145,210p' llp/3000.011.003.000.000-packaged-proof-kit.rfc.md && sed -n '620,655p' llp/3000.009-general-purpose-backend.rfc.md && rg -n '"'^### 3.4|''^### 3.5'"' llp/3000.019-what-the-builder-still-wrote.rfc.md" in /home/ccheever/projects/sb4-b18-dx4
 succeeded in 0ms:
snapback4 prune <dir> runs through the serving owner when live, otherwise the offline owner. It lists dormant tables, columns and indexes with row counts, logical serialized dormant bytes (including retained asset bytes separately) and at most five ids; it changes nothing without --allow-destructive. With that flag it permanently deletes exactly the currently dormant storage in one writer transaction, records a durable prune event, and leaves active rows/programs untouched. It releases dormant asset homes for the ordinary collector. Deploy/rollback --allow-destructive do not prune; direct users to the separate command. Prune intentionally deletes all dormant items as one reviewed storage boundary; per-item dependency-aware pruning is deferred because partial pruning can strand dormant references and asset homes. There is no implicit size or age collector: previews make storage growth visible without inventing a data-loss policy. After prune, reintroducing the schema gets empty tables and normal required-column/default behavior. Pruning is an irreversible operator action; the command output names every item dropped.

Release activation events live in the database transaction with activation revision, operation, release/previous/generation, timestamp and retained/restored summary. Prune records its item summary in that transaction. Existing releases.jsonl and current remain checked convenience copies; post-commit filesystem errors explicitly say activation succeeded. History is never edited or rewound. Rollback picks an earlier immutable source, not a historical data snapshot.

Dormant bytes have no automatic age-based deletion: retention lasts until explicit prune, and prune preview exposes populations so operators control disk use. Dormant asset homes remain live in the asset ledger but are unreadable because the active schema has no home. Active-row deletion also retires any associated dormant asset home. Dormant rows do not run old expiry or jobs while dormant; candidate liveness applies again upon reactivation. Explicit application deletion and ordinary active expiry still mean deletion, not an undo archive.

## 5. Hot activation and ownership

serve owns the database sidecar for its lifetime and publishes a Unix-domain release-control socket in the private per-user runtime directory (§5.1; directory 0700, socket 0600). Check peer credentials on both ends (Linux SO_PEERCRED, Darwin getpeereid), requiring the effective UID. Only the host filesystem owner has this capability; it is not an HTTP door, browser origin, bearer service seat or remote endpoint. Check filesystem objects before replacing a stale socket; never follow symlinks or replace a regular file. Use `.release-command` (a hardened private regular file, no-follow, single link) for the command flock and the deterministic runtime address (§5.1) for the control socket. lstat socket entries; only a same-owner socket may be removed while holding the exclusive store sidecar. Bound command JSON to 4096 bytes and response JSON to 64 KiB, with a 5-second input timeout and 120-second command response timeout. The trusted same-UID operator can directly edit source/database; malicious same-UID edits are outside the threat model, but ordinary source races are caught by a candidate content digest over source/native/worker files in the request and verified by the server. Numeric release id is the only source selector; never a caller path. Commands hold a separate private command flock while allocating/staging immutable release source. They acquire the store owner when stopped; E_STORE_BUSY with a running serve uses the local socket instead of opening another writer. Dev and an older/unreachable server continue to refuse with an exact stop/retry command.

snapback4 releases <dir> reads authoritative current release, activation revision, generation and durable events without adopting source, including while serve is live. This is the recovery command for an ambiguous result. A bounded command message identifies the owned database inode/canonical identity, candidate release id, expected current release and monotone activation revision, operation and explicit flags. The server compiles only its own immutable release directory, prepares native module/worker prerequisites before commit, then holds its existing engine writer guard for activation and in-memory publication. The server verifies both expected current release and activation revision inside the activation transaction. Increment activation revision on every successful release activation, including an equal-hash source change; A→B→A cannot satisfy an old command. Concurrent commands serialize on the command flock; out-of-band stale commands fail compare-and-swap. No request may choose arbitrary filesystem source. Compilation/validation failure leaves the previous engine, bound schema, native module, worker revision and reader factory unchanged.

A query already running in a pinned SQLite read transaction finishes on its old snapshot and reports that generation. A writer already executing finishes before activation obtains its engine guard. Queued HTTP operations are admitted under one release request guard covering signature lookup, argument decoding and execution. Activation acquires its exclusive side before taking the engine guard; admitted operations finish before activation, and later operations dispatch under the new generation. Internal detached readers still fence obsolete identities with E_GENERATION. Fresh requests after publication use the new engine; a stale pooled reader not yet pinned retries through the replacement factory. Prepare a runtime bundle containing bound programs, precompiled native module/metadata, worker root and validated named declarations; prepare the reader factory from the post-activation engine while its writer guard blocks new writers (reader opening failure after commit clears the old factory and uses existing lazy retry, never aborts a committed activation or advertises the old backend). Swap bound/generation/native under the common bound guard in the existing /schema guard order, retire pooled readers, rebind ephemeral state, invalidate change answers, wake sync/polls and update worker revision while holding the writer guard. The supervisor explicitly switches its immutable release project root and named declarations on revision, including addition/removal/replacement; externally supplied --worker stays an operator-owned entry. Worker preparation captures an immutable (root, entry, declarations/environment, supervisor revision) tuple. Under the engine guard it compares that captured revision to the current worker revision before minting credentials; stale preparation is discarded and rediscovered. Never relabel an old prepared source with the latest revision. Credentials bind that checked revision so later activation fences them even when spawn finishes later. Workers restart using the candidate packaged source and native continuation hashes keep the existing conservative parking policy. Long validation can pause writes; hot means no process restart, not an unbounded zero-pause latency promise.

The server continues an admitted activation even if the client hits its 120-second response timeout or closes the socket; failure to write the reply is not permission to roll back or exit the server. The database is authoritative at crash or lost socket reply. Timeout/EOF after sending a command reports an ambiguous result and instructs inspection of current and durable history; never silently resend as a new release or claim failure left the old release active. Before activation fsync required immutable source/native/worker files and their directories, then the published releases directory entry. The supported guarantee covers process crashes and filesystem/SQLite durability on hosts honoring fsync; no guarantee covers failing storage hardware. Restart attaches the database-selected release even if current's convenience copy is stale. After database commit, publication is infallible in-memory assignments and committed ephemeral rebind; fallible native compilation, declaration/environment/import validation happens before commit. Worker process spawn remains fallible after commit: failure reports committed activation with degraded worker availability and retries; it cannot undo a release or external effects. Filesystem-copy failures report the committed result.

### 5.1 Path-independent control address

The accept loop polls a nonblocking listener, but immediately resets each
accepted stream to blocking mode before peer validation or input. Darwin/BSD
inherits the listener's nonblocking flag; Linux does not. Blocking streams
let the existing total input deadline govern clients that write after accept.
If resetting the stream fails, close it and report the accept error; never
admit a command on a stream with unknown I/O mode.

Amendment commissioned and implemented by Codex for Charlie on 2026-10-12,
for the 0.4.24 macOS publication blocker. Darwin's Unix socket path limit
is 104 bytes; the previous deployment-local name failed under ordinary
`/var/folders/.../T/` fixtures. Linux's descriptor-address workaround did not
solve Darwin or provide one portable transport.

Use `/tmp/snapback4-<effective-uid>/<sha256>.sock` on both hosts. The hash is
SHA-256 over the raw bytes of the canonical **deployment directory**, binding
the socket to immutable release source. Aliases that canonicalize to the same path resolve
to one address; two deployments pointing to one external database retain
separate addresses, so only the serving deployment can submit its releases.
At deploy and serve startup, create a missing deployment privately, resolve
its canonical directory once, then use that directory for ownership and all
release paths. Serve also resolves the selected release root before constructing
the project. Worker cwd and `SNAPBACK_PROJECT_DIR` therefore report the same
canonical active release path, including Darwin's `/var` → `/private/var` and
explicit deployment symlinks. An existing deployment alias resolves before
private-directory validation; no symlink is installed inside private storage.
Use the same canonical path spelling on case/normalization-insensitive hosts;
byte-distinct canonical spellings produce distinct addresses. The request
envelope still binds the canonical database path, device and inode.
The longest address is 95 bytes (ten decimal UID digits), below both limits.
Do not use TMPDIR or XDG_RUNTIME_DIR: these may be long or differ between CLI
invocations. This deterministic address needs no durable locator file; deploy
and rollback derive exactly the same address from durable deployment location.
A moved deployment must stop and restart serve, as moving its immutable source
already requires. Socket entries are ephemeral, never backup authority.

Create the single runtime directory atomically as 0700. Open with O_DIRECTORY
and O_NOFOLLOW, inspect the opened metadata, and refuse a different UID, a
non-directory/symlink or any mode other than 0700 (including special bits).
socket for the next exclusive owner. A crash between bind and chmod may leave
a socket with the launching umask's mode; if it is not exactly 0600, recovery
refuses and the operator must remove that stale entry. Do not change the
process-global umask in a multithreaded host to hide this fail-closed window. Keep the shared runtime directory on exit:
removing it would race other deployments, and it contains no durable state.

Acceptance: default-TMPDIR project paths exceeding 108 bytes on Linux and
Darwin start serve, execute hot deploy and rollback without restarting, refuse
a second host, clean up on SIGTERM, and restart after SIGKILL. Unit regressions
cover stale/live socket entries, file/symlink and 0600 socket refusals, directory
permissions/type/UID checks, canonical aliases, independent deployments, and
preserving replaced entries during cleanup. SIGTERM/SIGINT/SIGHUP also drain
an incomplete control request before process exit; join the control handle
while Shared still retains the owner. An admitted activation is allowed to
finish. Enforce the five-second input bound as a total monotonic deadline,
updating the remaining socket read timeout before every read; partial input
arriving repeatedly cannot reset the deadline or prolong shutdown. The signal
regression keeps sending partial input until the host closes the connection.
No budget or public door changes.

## 6. Devices and partitions

Every changed execution activation including A→B→A gets a fresh monotone semantic generation, never reuses a release number as a generation. Execution-content identity includes the prepared native module digest as well as schema/programs. A changed native digest advances the existing semantic generation atomically with release activation, even when native declarations are unchanged; removing a module changes the digest too. Equal execution content retains generation unless a release activation changes the final active/dormant population (§9.1), which requires a new replica generation; source-only activations still advance activation revision. The digest is SHA-256 over the exact prepared wasm bytes; the no-module sentinel is the empty string persisted as release_native_digest. activation_hash binds schema/programs and that digest. Comparison is with the current activation, so A→B→A is fresh. Startup attaches the persisted execution identity and verifies the immutable release module matches its recorded digest. This uses the existing device protocol, not a separate native epoch. Rollback and forward use the same generation replacement and full partition reacquisition already used by adoption: /schema advertises the exact active shape, sync rejects old watermarks, devices clear obsolete base/index state and rebase or refuse their queued intents under the existing authority/operation contract. Store id remains unchanged; this is not a store replacement. No dormant rows or fields enter snapshots, partition logs, online answers or device stores. Never combine old-generation pages with a new backend. A late old read remains tagged old and cannot overwrite a newly observed generation. Simulate rollback/forward across a delayed sync page and audience changes; retained data changes server storage only, not device persistence formats.

Lock order: command flock is CLI-only; serving control never acquires it. Candidate filesystem/native/worker preparation precedes engine lock. Serving publication holds engine → bound → generation → native, and separately replaces readers while engine is held (matching existing checkout); no /schema handler takes engine while holding bound. Ephemeral rebind uses its existing committed infallible path, never a fallible post-commit store read. Update desired worker root before revision; worker thread releases root/bound guards before taking engine. Wake/seq/answers follow existing publish_engine while engine is held: these are short memory updates/nonblocking condition notifications, required before new writer admission; no socket/child wait occurs there. `releases` includes the existing worker status metadata so post-commit launch failure is visible as degraded availability. No SQLite lock is held while waiting for a child process or socket peer.

## 7. Implementation and acceptance

Small commits: reviewed design; transactional retention/prune/refusals; local hot-control publication; integration/device/client coverage and docs. Authoring entry: SERVER.md owns the release-lifecycle section; guide evolution owns schema interactions. OPS guide deploy gets a one-line pointer to that section.

CLI matrix: `deploy <dir> [--restore-dormant] [--allow-feed-adoption]`; `rollback <dir> [--to N] [--allow-feed-adoption]`; `prune <dir> [--allow-destructive]`; `releases <dir>`. Legacy --allow-destructive on deploy/rollback is accepted but does not prune or bypass validation, and prints the prune command when relevant. --restore-dormant authorizes only restoration, never loss. --to requires a positive existing immutable release id and may select a newer release. E_ADOPT operator text: `table.column/index: N affected rows; sample ids [...]; values/keys [...]; reason; rewrite: <bridge/repair schema or command>; inspect: snapback4 sql <query> --release <dir>`. No flag bypasses invalid values, ownership or references. Unreachable live control refuses `E_STORE_BUSY: stop snapback4 serve <dir>, rerun the exact command, then snapback4 serve <dir>` before send; after send use `releases <dir>` and explicit `rollback --to N` after inspecting the outcome, never an automatic deploy allocating N+1.

History/status is page-based with `releases <dir> [--after <event-id>]`; bounded pages return a continuation and inventory pages use stable item keys. Prune audit stores a complete item list in SQLite; bounded replies carry a continuation rather than truncate silently. Critical assertions include T(a,b) → absent T → T(a) → absent T → T(a,b), preserved b and latest updated a; identical parent id/value replacement invalidates dormant edges; stale revision after A→B→A and equal-hash activation refuses; activation-time parent expiry followed by identical-id replacement invalidates dormant references; rapid B→C activation with a barrier between B worker preparation and credential minting discards B conventional/named source; an old checked-out reader not yet pinned retries the new factory including native-only changes; a connected client holding native module A hot-adopts B with unchanged declarations, reacquires B backend/module and refuses a delayed A result; inject interruption before/after commit and during pointer-copy updates to distinguish no activation from committed activation with an ambiguous result.

Tests also cover private socket permissions/symlinks/peer credentials, racing commands and writes during validation, process crash before commit and between commit/publication/reply, live prune/status, legacy empty dormancy and unchanged device format. Tests: deploy v1/write, v2 adding table/required default/enum/index/write, precise refusal while new enum is in use, authorized repair then rollback with dormant table/column, old update/insert/delete, forward restores prior values and supplies defaults only to new rows. Also prune preview/confirmation and asset lifetime; unique failure after dormant-index writes; required default-less forward refusal; transaction failure leaves active and dormant data intact; restart binds the committed release; simultaneous hot requests and failed hot activation; stale-generation sync and real client reacquisition; simulator delayed pages/audiences. Quality bar: the commissioned core/lang/device/sim cargo suites, CLI release/serve/adoption tests, relevant client tests and ref-check. No data-loss claim rests on diagnostics alone.

# LLP 3000.011.003.000.000: Packaged application proof kit

**Type:** RFC
**Status:** Implemented
**Systems:** Snapback 4 proof tooling, starters, fixtures, release source selection
**Author:** Codex for Charlie Cheever
**Date:** 2026-10-14
**Revised:** 2026-10-18 (DAY5 amendments; KIT4 clock, proof and test contracts); 2026-10-17 (KIT3 proof and clock contracts); 2026-10-16 (REG missing frontend export diagnostic; §11: wave-14 DAY4); 2026-10-16 (§9: wave-13 DAY3; §10: wave-13 KIT2); 2026-10-16 (§8: explicit platform boundary and client-source guard); 2026-10-15 (§8: wave-12 KIT and observational listener qualification)
**Related:** [Starters](./3000.011.003.000-offline-ready-application-starters.rfc.md), [Release activation](./3000.000.000-retained-storage-and-hot-release.rfc.md), [Built application proof](./3000.011.005.000-qualify-the-built-application.rfc.md)

STAGE staging and persistent startDev defaults are amended by [LLP 3000.000.000.001](./3000.000.000.001-staging-with-real-users.rfc.md); KIT4 owns other ongoing helper changes.

## 1. Contract

Implementer: PROOF workstream, 2026-10-14. Wave 11 fix list 1 authorizes
one published `snapback4/proof` Node module. It owns orchestration, never client
custody or backend/bundler supervision in the product. Existing `startDev(args)`
and `stopChild(child | {url, stop})` recipes remain usable as module exports.

`startDev`, `startServe(directory)`, `startStatic(directory)` and
`startVite(directory)` return `{url, pid, logs, stop}`. Options carry cwd,
environment, arguments and bounded ready/stop deadlines. Read PORT_LO/PORT_HI
at invocation: absent means an OS-assigned port; exactly one set refuses. With
both set, validate 1 <= LO <= HI <= 65535 and bind the first free port in that
inclusive range; exhaustion refuses. Qualification's occupied first port must
be skipped, never adopted. The existing native SNAPBACK4_READY line already names its direct child PID (LLP 3000.011.004.000); no new readiness log is needed. The listening process binds first and keeps that socket;
never adopt an already-serving port. Vite uses its supported JavaScript server
API in a directly spawned Node child, strict ports, the project's config and
a per-launch identity middleware. The directly spawned frontend calls its
server listen API for each candidate; no probe-and-release step exists. With
no range it calls listen on port 0, which the tested Vite API supports with
strictPort; it reads the actual retained socket address. Static hosting uses a directly spawned Node
child with traversal protection, correct wasm MIME and SPA fallback.

All child stdout/stderr go to separate files beneath `logs/`. Read complete
lines from those files, strip ANSI, bound readiness, and reject premature exit.
Native ready lines must name the directly spawned PID. To assert HTTP identity,
the kit sets a random SNAPBACK4_PROOF_ID only in the child environment;
`/auth/config` includes `proof_id` only when this variable is nonempty. It
confers no authority or bypass, and ordinary servers omit it. Both static and Vite frontend child
responses carry an `x-snapback4-proof` marker. Identity validation
precedes returning a handle. No public development door is enabled in serve.

Register every spawned child and browser process for an exit-time SIGKILL
sweep, using retained native ChildProcess handles, not process-name kills.
TERM followed by bounded KILL/reap stops a child. Every cleanup action has
its own bound and later actions still run when an earlier action fails.
`cli(args)` runs the native binary, awaits close, returns separate stdout and
stderr, and has a command deadline. It never runs npm/npx or shell strings.

`withBrowser({executablePath: process.env.CHROME}, callback)` launches Chromium
through playwright-core, closes with a bound, and registers its actual browser
process. Root needs --no-sandbox; other hosts first attempt a sandboxed launch
and retry without it only on recognized sandbox-unavailable diagnostics: "No usable sandbox", "Running as
root without --no-sandbox", "Failed to move to new namespace", "SUID sandbox",
"sandbox ... Operation not permitted", or "Failed to unshare ... Operation not
permitted". Unrelated failures never retry.
`waitForFresh` polls for nonempty changed text; `fillRN` clears and types real
keystrokes; `statusText` uses text selectors; `selectByValue` selects stable
option values. The browser executable is CHROME (when provided), undefined executablePath is omitted from launch options, then Playwright's
installed Chromium is used; an absent executable refuses at launch, with its path.
waitForFresh defaults to 30 seconds. Binary resolution is resolveBinary from
the same installed snapback4 package (override SNAPBACK4_BIN only for explicit
workspace tests); the opt-in identity requires CLI/client 0.4.28 or newer and
an older CLI omits proof_id and therefore fails the exact marker assertion; no inferred version comparison or success claim is made. cli(args) does not
invoke package managers; publication uses its existing guarded command runner.

None interprets an HTTP acknowledgement as rendered settlement.

## 2. Explicit source and aliases
and execution results. Guide testing replaces orchestration recipes with the
kit and four rules: file logs; fresh signals; receipts outside unmounting
components; HTTP acknowledgement is not React settlement.

NARR owns deploy/rollback narration; DX3 owns general client recipes and discovery.

## 4. Custody scope and review disposition

The registered process set uses retained handles. Static and Vite Node hosts
start in private Unix groups and launch a live Node keeper in that same group.
The host's stdin is a driver-owned liveness pipe; driver death including SIGKILL
produces EOF and terminates the host. The keeper's stdin belongs only to the
host. Host exit (including SIGKILL)
closes it; the keeper signals its own group with SIGKILL. The driver gives the host only the read end of its liveness pipe; the keeper
likewise receives only its pipe's read end. Parent write ends are close-on-exec
and never appear in child stdio or IPC descriptors; compiler stdin is its own
pipe, never these write ends. Thus descendant inheritance cannot retain a
liveness writer after its owner dies. The keeper's live membership
pins the group identity, so no observer signals a recycled negative PID.
Vite closes its compiler before normal host exit; the keeper sweeps anything
remaining after either normal or forced exit. On Windows, direct-child cleanup
remains supported; private Unix-group custody is not claimed. Browser processes
use Playwright's process ownership and close before forced stop. Native servers use their existing SNAPBACK4_SUPERVISOR_PID parent-death contract
with the proof driver's PID and already own worker cleanup. Arbitrary detached application processes are outside
this module's supported custody. Publication's existing live-keeper groups remain
the outer command owner and enforce drained groups after each proof, including
abrupt driver death. No process-name or unrelated PID kills are allowed.

## 5. Exact helper defaults

Each launch uses an independent crypto.randomUUID marker. Native children receive
that marker as SNAPBACK4_PROOF_ID; the parent requires exact equality in
/auth/config.proof_id. Frontend children receive their launch UUID, and the
parent requires the exact pid:UUID header, never mere presence. Children in a
scenario need not share a marker: independent markers prove each handle's owner.
Native hosts receive --port LO-HI (or --port 0); only the child binds and prints
its actual loopback URL. The frontend child binds internally, never by a dropped
probe. Defaults: ready 30 s; TERM grace 5 s; KILL reap 5 s; CLI/command 120 s;
each cleanup 10 s; locator/navigation/fresh signal 30 s; browser/context close
5 s each. Log files have fresh UUID names below cwd/logs, never reused.

startServe serves an immutable deployed backend directory; startStatic serves
built frontend files, not a database. command uses a caller-selected executable
and argv (normal spawn PATH lookup when not absolute), shell:false semantics,
file logs and close/deadline checks; cli uses the package resolver. Use direct
tool binaries, never npm/npx to launch proof servers. Standard starter proof
reads identity/personas from its owned dev at run time, chooses guest/password/
persona sign-in and proves the visible todo add when Task exists. With a custom
Home, it proves sign-in/readiness only; the emitted instructions require the
builder's own business assertions after replacing the screen.

Fixture substitution recursively visits JSON arrays/object values. Alias lookup
is exact and case-sensitive. A $ prefix parses the name before the first dot,
then traverses each exact field; no dot returns a saved row's id. $$ strips one
leading dollar and is terminal (no re-expansion). A sole-key {"$literal":value}
is terminal and returns value untouched. Bare saved-value argument strings matching the saved
map refuse (fixture contracts §7); the wire decoder never receives or consults that map. All other
strings, including a dollar substring, stay literal. Normal Vite/compiler
children remain in the host's private group and receive separate stdin; daemonized
processes leaving it are outside custody. The keeper handles EOF with immediate
SIGKILL of its own group; the host handles driver EOF with immediate exit. Parent
write descriptors never pass to either child, so descendants cannot hold them.
Windows has direct-child stop/exit cleanup only; no abrupt-death group guarantee.

## 6. Source and discovery boundaries

mutation, route and change-poll authentication apply the same revision fence as
claims, within the live-session snapshot, including any older refreshed family members.
`/auth/refresh` refuses supervised families with HTTP 403 `E_WORKER_REFRESH`,
after rechecking liveness under the serving engine guard. The supervisor alone
rotates these tokens: issuing a successor through HTTP would create a bearer
outside its log redactors' custody. Refusal leaves the injected bearer live.
Both named and conventional workers follow this rule. Manually provisioned seats,
even for the same service principal, retain ordinary liveness and refresh rules.
A pipe-owned watchdog launches each Node worker in its own process group.
It inherits the host's store ownership descriptor and retains the same lock
until group cleanup finishes, including after host death. The descriptor closes
on Node execution; application processes never hold it. A replacement `dev` or
`serve` waits up to five seconds for ownership before opening the store or starting
workers, then reports `E_STORE_BUSY` if ownership remains held. A reachable
announced loopback listener or an unannounced CLI owner refuses immediately;
only an announced host without a reachable listener gets the cleanup wait.
The address is only a wait/refusal hint: startup always requires the actual
sidecar lock. Non-loopback addresses are never dialed. Other commands retain
immediate busy-store refusal. The ownership sidecar must remain in place
(LLP 3000.019 §3.4); fresh dev stores remain independent.
The server alone holds the pipe's writer: EOF on ordinary shutdown or abrupt
death (including SIGKILL) triggers group termination on Linux and macOS.
Worker exit also kills the group, so helpers cannot retain ports or ledger
locks. Termination allows up to two seconds for cleanup, then kills remaining
group members. Shutdown starts all workers' grace periods before waiting for
any of them. Mint, secret-load, retirement and spawn failures all use increasing
backoff bounded at five seconds. Deliberate process-group escape by trusted
application code is not sandboxed. Public PID/status refers to Node, not its
watchdog. If the watchdog itself exits by signal (including SIGKILL/OOM),
the host kills the retained Node process group before forgetting its PID or
starting a replacement. Catchable signals sent directly to the watchdog
(TERM/INT/HUP) initiate its bounded group cleanup, so a host-wide termination
cannot destroy the cleanup owner before it kills descendants. If a newly added
declaration fails initial admission (for example, Node is temporarily unavailable),
the supervisor retains its name and retries with the same bounded backoff,
rediscovering valid source each time. Repair does not require another source edit.
368:### 3.4 P8: residency you can see, and a CLI that does not fight the dev server
489:### 3.5 P9: a list of media is a column

exec
/bin/bash -lc "sed -n '368,490p' llp/3000.019-what-the-builder-still-wrote.rfc.md && cat snapback4/crates/snapback4/src/store_owner.rs" in /home/ccheever/projects/sb4-b18-dx4
 succeeded in 0ms:
### 3.4 P8: residency you can see, and a CLI that does not fight the dev server

**Change.**

- `client.residency()` returns rows and bytes per table on the device, with
  the store's total bytes and, on the web, the wasm heap. The guide's residency
  card shows it next to the sync windows that bound it.
- `snapback4 run <job>` and the other store-writing CLI commands detect a live
  dev server on the same store (its lifetime lock file) and either run the work
  through that server or refuse with `E_STORE_BUSY`, naming the server's
  address, instead of failing with `database is locked`. Which of the two is
  the lane's first finding.

**Evidence.** Residency on a fixture with known rows; the CLI against a running
dev server, both ways.

**2026-10-08 — `run` goes through a live dev host (app-farm round 1).** The
lane's first finding was the refusal; 27 of ~270 app-farm builds (41 diaries)
then stopped their dev server to run one operation, or reverse-engineered the
wire. `run` now takes the other branch of this item when the owner is a
responsive `snapback4 dev` (it answers `GET /dev/status`): a query or mutation
goes to `POST /q|/m/<op>` with the persona header naming the same principal a
local run would use, and a job to the dev-only `POST /dev/jobs/<job>`, which
queues it behind earlier runs and drives chunks on the dev clock. No second
writer opens the store. `--now`, a non-persona principal, `serve` and a stopped
host still refuse `E_STORE_BUSY`, now with the equivalent `curl`. Other
store-writing commands keep the refusal.

**2026-09-27 — Darwin ownership correction (lane OW).** Ownership now locks
`<database filename>-owner` beside the canonical database, a regular 0600 file
opened with `O_NOFOLLOW`: exclusive for backend writers, shared for devices
including the native C ABI. On macOS/iOS, a flock on the database itself
conflicts with SQLite's POSIX locks, preventing startup adoption and device
commits. Database identity remains `(dev, ino)`, checked with stat alone;
only a missing database is created and closed before SQLite opens it. The
guard holds the sidecar, never a database descriptor whose close could discard
SQLite's locks. Sidecars persist after unlock and move with the database's
other sidecars; symlinks, hard links and nonregular ownership files are refused.
`serve.lock` remains an address hint, and `E_STORE_BUSY` keeps its meaning.

**2026-09-27 — round-1 file-mutation hardening (lane OW).** Both ownership
and address-file opens lstat the database and candidate before opening and
compare the opened inode again before any lock, read, write or chmod. A
post-open alias or uncertain descriptor is deliberately forgotten, never
closed or unlocked: either operation could discard SQLite's POSIX locks.
Ownership sidecars must be empty, and address records at most 128 bytes;
these stat-only bounds also refuse a SQLite database renamed to either name
when its original path is absent. Lock the sidecar before creating a missing
database; an `E_STORE_BUSY` probe creates no database or address record.
Database hard links are refused with `E_STORE` and “copy the store, do not
hard-link it,” including on an existing owner's path check.

**2026-09-27 — round-2 exclusion boundary (lane OW, option b).** Exclusion
holds while `<database>-owner` is left in place. Deleting or replacing it while
a process runs ends exclusion: a second owner can create and lock a new sidecar
while the first can continue reading and writing. Removal does not revoke a
running process or cancel its transactions. The retained descriptor still locks
the old inode, which no longer coordinates new opens. Stop all owners before
moving a store and move its sidecars with it; leave `-owner` in place for the
whole ownership lifetime.

This replaces round 1's partial fail-closed claim and removes its live sidecar
checks. `Store::conn()` exposes a raw SQLite connection: auth autocommit writes,
auth and worker transactions, and effect/action/job/timeline reads bypass the
store's transaction helpers. There is no single existing fallible interception
point; changing that accessor's return type would change the connection API and
its callers, and an accessor check cannot revoke a previously borrowed
connection. No single-digit-microsecond transaction-cost claim is made. The
database inode, hard-link and acquisition-time sidecar checks remain. Unit,
native-device and two-process regressions label unlink/replacement as this
documented boundary and prove a second owner can start while old work continues.

**2026-09-27 — live token mutual proof (lane TK, round 1).** A busy
`token` command asks the announced owner to mint through its existing writer.
The per-run 0600 `<canonical database>-token` secret never crosses HTTP:
BSD wildcard listeners need not own the specific loopback address the CLI
dials. Both rounds require an actual loopback socket peer, one literal
loopback Host, no Origin and single protocol headers. IPv4-mapped IPv6 peers
fail closed, including mapped loopback; native `::1` remains usable.

The client sends a fresh 32-byte OS nonce and the request. The server issues a
fresh 32-byte nonce and HMAC-SHA256 over `snapback4-token-server`, client nonce,
server nonce and request binding. Only after verifying it does the client send
HMAC-SHA256 over `snapback4-token-client`, server nonce, client nonce and the
same binding. Nonces are fixed-width raw bytes within the MAC, hex on the wire;
the HMAC key is the exact 64 ASCII hex bytes in the private file. The binding
is UTF-8 JSON of `[1, service, days, storeIdentity]`; days bind before clamping.
The identity hashes JSON of `[hex(canonicalPathBytes), device, inode]`, avoiding
`/var` versus `/private/var` aliases and lossy path encoding. Different stores
also have independent per-run secrets.

The server holds at most 128 pending nonces, expiring after 10 monotonic seconds;
completion atomically removes the nonce before verifying its MAC or minting.
Both sides use RustCrypto HMAC's constant-time `verify_slice`, never string
comparison of tags. Malformed/expired/wrong proofs and boundary failures have
one 403 refusal, no CORS, no secret/token diagnostics. Body/reply limits stay
4,096/8,192 bytes; there is no automatic retry or offline fallback after live
failure. A lost final reply can leave one unused session until expiry.

Mutual MACs alone would let an active impostor forward the handshake and read
the final bearer. Thus the one final reply (including authenticated validation
errors) uses ChaCha20-Poly1305, with a key derived by HMAC under the separate
`snapback4-token-reply` domain, client nonce, server nonce and binding, the
first 12 server-nonce bytes as its AEAD nonce, and binding as associated data.
The single-use server nonce permits exactly one encryption under this derived
key. A relay can forward the caller's one intended mint or deny delivery;
it cannot read the resulting bearer or mint another by replay. Offline
validation, session storage and wall-clock expiry remain the same identity
routine. These dependencies belong only to the server/CLI, outside the device.

**2026-09-26 — Darwin qualification (lane DQ).** Residency counters and
triggers join successful device installation's transaction. Reopening an
unchanged legacy backend computes missing counters in SQLite temp storage;
it does not upgrade the old database before a later adoption can refuse.
Temp triggers maintain this connection's writes; another owner's commit
invalidates the cache through `data_version` and the next inspection rebuilds
it in one read snapshot. Repeated inspections without peer commits do not
scan the partition. A successful new installation or backend adoption
promotes counters to durable storage and removes the temp triggers. Neither
capability refusal nor inspection changes the legacy SQLite bytes.

### 3.5 P9: a list of media is a column

//! Ownership locks the database's sidecar; stat pins its inode. `serve.lock` is only an address hint.
use std::{fs::{self, File}, io::{Read, Seek, SeekFrom, Write}, path::{Path, PathBuf}, time::Duration};
use snapback4_core::{Engine, RuntimeError, Store};
#[cfg(unix)]
use std::os::unix::fs::MetadataExt;

pub(crate) struct Owner {
    _sidecar: std::sync::Arc<File>,
    identity: (u64, u64),
    path: PathBuf,
    record: Option<File>,
    reclaimed: Option<String>,
    pub(crate) token_authority: Option<crate::serve::token::Authority>,
}

/// The address `doctor` dials for the server that owns this store: the
/// published loopback address itself, or, for a wildcard bind, that family's
/// loopback on the same port. A published non-loopback address is not followed.
pub(crate) fn inspector_address(store: &Path) -> Option<std::net::SocketAddr> {
    let path = store.canonicalize().ok()?;
    dialable(&address(&path)?)
}

fn dialable(published: &str) -> Option<std::net::SocketAddr> {
    use std::net::{IpAddr, Ipv4Addr, Ipv6Addr, SocketAddr};
    let address = published.strip_prefix("http://")?.parse::<SocketAddr>().ok()?;
    let ip = match address.ip() {
        ip if ip.is_loopback() => ip,
        IpAddr::V4(ip) if ip.is_unspecified() => IpAddr::V4(Ipv4Addr::LOCALHOST),
        IpAddr::V6(ip) if ip.is_unspecified() => IpAddr::V6(Ipv6Addr::LOCALHOST),
        _ => return None,
    };
    (address.port() != 0).then(|| SocketAddr::new(ip, address.port()))
}

fn resolve(store: &Path) -> Result<PathBuf, String> {
    match store.canonicalize() {
        Ok(path) => Ok(path),
        Err(e) if e.kind() == std::io::ErrorKind::NotFound => {
            let parent = store.parent().filter(|p| !p.as_os_str().is_empty()).unwrap_or(Path::new("."));
            snapback4_core::private_fs::directory(parent).map_err(|e| failure(store, e))?;
            Ok(parent.canonicalize().map_err(|e| failure(store, e))?.join(store.file_name().ok_or("store path has no filename")?))
        }
        Err(e) => Err(failure(store, e)),
    }
}

fn failure(path: &Path, reason: impl std::fmt::Display) -> String {
    format!("E_STORE at {}: {reason}; restore a regular store/address file and retry", path.display())
}

#[cfg(unix)]
fn same_file(a: &fs::Metadata, b: &fs::Metadata) -> bool { a.dev() == b.dev() && a.ino() == b.ino() }

#[cfg(unix)]
fn regular(path: &Path, create: bool, database: &Path) -> Result<File, String> {
    snapback4_core::private_fs::auxiliary_file(database, path, create, 128)
        .map_err(|e| failure(path, e))
}

// @ref LLP 3000.019#3.4 — sidecar flocks fence backend writers and share device ownership without locking SQLite.
pub(crate) fn command(store: &Path) -> Result<Owner, String> { acquire(store, false, Duration::ZERO, true) }
pub(crate) fn device(store: &Path) -> Result<Owner, String> { acquire(store, true, Duration::ZERO, true) }
// @ref LLP 3000.009#5.3 — a restarting host waits for watchdogs to finish group cleanup.
pub(crate) fn host(store: &Path) -> Result<Owner, String> { acquire(store, false, Duration::from_secs(5), true) }

// @ref LLP 3000.000.000.001#9-wave-19-identity-retry-and-local-production-lifecycle — only pre-admission ownership can wait; a live host keeps its control path.
pub(crate) fn release_command(store: &Path) -> Result<Owner, String> {
    let live = address(store).is_some_and(|published| dialable(&published).is_none_or(|address|
        std::net::TcpStream::connect_timeout(&address, Duration::from_millis(50)).is_ok()));
    wait_command(store, if live { Duration::ZERO } else { Duration::from_secs(5) })
}
pub(crate) fn wait_command(store: &Path, wait: Duration) -> Result<Owner, String> {
    acquire(store, false, wait, false)
}

/// Read-only release barrier: never initializes SQLite, creates a lock file,
/// clears an address, or treats a PID/listener hint as authority.
#[cfg(unix)]
pub(crate) fn wait_released(store: &Path, wait: Duration) -> Result<(), String> {
    let path = store.canonicalize().map_err(|e| failure(store, e))?;
    let before = snapback4_core::private_fs::database_metadata(&path).map_err(|e| failure(&path,e))?
        .ok_or_else(|| failure(&path,"store disappeared"))?;
    let sidecar = match snapback4_core::private_fs::auxiliary_file(&path, &snapback4_core::private_fs::owner_path(&path), false, 0) {
        Ok(file) => file,
        Err(e) if e.kind() == std::io::ErrorKind::NotFound => return Ok(()),
        Err(e) => return Err(failure(&path,e)),
    };
    let end = std::time::Instant::now() + wait;
    loop {
        match sidecar.try_lock() {
            Ok(()) => break,
            Err(fs::TryLockError::WouldBlock) if std::time::Instant::now() < end => std::thread::sleep(Duration::from_millis(25)),
            Err(fs::TryLockError::WouldBlock) => return Err(busy(&path)),
            Err(fs::TryLockError::Error(e)) => return Err(failure(&path,e)),
        }
    }
    let after = snapback4_core::private_fs::database_metadata(&path).map_err(|e|failure(&path,e))?;
    if !snapback4_core::private_fs::owner_is_named(&path,&sidecar) || !after.is_some_and(|after|same_file(&before,&after)) {
        return Err(failure(&path,"store or ownership sidecar changed while waiting"));
    }
    Ok(())
}

#[cfg(not(unix))]
pub(crate) fn wait_released(store: &Path, _: Duration) -> Result<(), String> { Err(failure(store,"database ownership requires Unix")) }

#[cfg(unix)]
fn acquire(store: &Path, shared: bool, wait: Duration, cleanup_only: bool) -> Result<Owner, String> {
    let deadline = std::time::Instant::now() + wait;
    for _ in 0..16 {
        let path = resolve(store)?;
        snapback4_core::private_fs::directory(path.parent().ok_or("store path has no parent")?)
            .map_err(|e| failure(&path, e))?;
        // Stat before opening either auxiliary file; a refused probe creates
        // no database. Only the sidecar lock grants permission to create one.
        let before = snapback4_core::private_fs::database_metadata(&path).map_err(|e| failure(&path, e))?;
        let sidecar = snapback4_core::private_fs::owner_file(&path)
            .map_err(|e| failure(&snapback4_core::private_fs::owner_path(&path), e))?;
        let mut cleanup = None;
        loop {
            let locked = if shared { sidecar.try_lock_shared() } else { sidecar.try_lock() };
            match locked {
                Ok(()) => break,
                Err(fs::TryLockError::WouldBlock) if std::time::Instant::now() < deadline && (!cleanup_only || *cleanup.get_or_insert_with(|| stopped_host(&path))) => {
                    if cleanup.is_none() { eprintln!("waiting for store owner cleanup: {} (up to {} ms); no work admitted", path.display(), wait.as_millis()); cleanup = Some(true); }
                    std::thread::sleep(Duration::from_millis(25));
                },
                Err(fs::TryLockError::WouldBlock) => return Err(busy(&path)),
                Err(fs::TryLockError::Error(error)) => return Err(failure(&path, error)),
            }
        }
        if !snapback4_core::private_fs::owner_is_named(&path, &sidecar) { continue; }
        snapback4_core::private_fs::database_file(&path).map_err(|e| failure(&path, e))?;
        let database = snapback4_core::private_fs::database_metadata(&path).map_err(|e| failure(&path, e))?
            .ok_or_else(|| failure(&path, "database disappeared during ownership acquisition"))?;
        if before.as_ref().is_some_and(|old| !same_file(old, &database)) { continue; }
        let mut reclaimed = None;
        let record = if shared { None } else {
            let record_path = path.with_file_name("serve.lock");
            if let Some(pid)=owner_pid(&path) {
                if unsafe {libc::kill(pid as libc::pid_t,0)} == -1 && std::io::Error::last_os_error().raw_os_error()==Some(libc::ESRCH) {
                    reclaimed = Some(format!("reclaimed stale serve.lock: dead PID {pid}; directory {}", path.parent().unwrap().display()));
                }
            }
            let file = regular(&record_path, true, &path)?;
            file.set_len(0).map_err(|e| failure(&record_path, e))?;
            Some(file)
        };
        return Ok(Owner { _sidecar: std::sync::Arc::new(sidecar), identity: (database.dev(), database.ino()), path, record, reclaimed, token_authority: None });
    }
    Err(failure(store, "database path kept changing during ownership acquisition"))
}

#[cfg(not(unix))]
fn acquire(store: &Path, _: bool, _: Duration, _: bool) -> Result<Owner, String> { Err(failure(store, "database ownership requires Unix")) }

pub(crate) fn stopped_host(path: &Path) -> bool {
    // A CLI owner or a host not yet announced still refuses immediately. A
    // reachable listener does too; only a prior host's cleanup gets a wait.
    // This is a refusal/wait hint, never permission to open the store: the
    // sidecar must still be acquired. Never dial a non-loopback address hint.
    address(path).is_some_and(|published| dialable(&published).is_none_or(|address|
        std::net::TcpStream::connect_timeout(&address, Duration::from_millis(50)).is_err()))
}

pub(crate) fn busy(path: &Path) -> String {
    let owner = address(path).map(|address| match owner_pid(path) { Some(pid) => format!("{address} (pid {pid})"), None => address })
        .unwrap_or_else(|| "another CLI command or a server starting up (address pending)".into());
    format!("E_STORE_BUSY at {}: this store is live; owned by {owner}; directory {}; no work was admitted. For a disposable parallel proof use `dev --memory` (startDev({{args:[\"--memory\"]}})); otherwise stop its `snapback4 dev` or `snapback4 serve` process (or wait for the owning CLI command), rerun this command once, then restart the server. Exclusion holds while <database>-owner is left in place; deleting or replacing it while a process runs ends exclusion and does not stop that process", path.display(),path.parent().unwrap_or(Path::new(".")).display())
}

#[cfg(unix)]
pub(crate) fn address(path: &Path) -> Option<String> {
    // A missing/replaced address record cannot grant ownership. Never follow
    // a symlink (including one to the database), nor block on a FIFO.
    let record = path.with_file_name("serve.lock");
    let file = regular(&record, false, path).ok()?;
    let mut text = String::new();
    file.take(1024).read_to_string(&mut text).ok()?;
    let mut lines = text.lines();
    let port = lines.next().and_then(|s| s.parse::<u16>().ok());
    lines.next().map(str::to_owned).or_else(|| port.map(|p| format!("http://127.0.0.1:{p}")))
}

/// The owning host's PID from its address record (`pid N` on the third line).
#[cfg(unix)]
fn owner_pid(path: &Path) -> Option<u32> {
    let record = path.with_file_name("serve.lock");
    let file = regular(&record, false, path).ok()?;
    let mut text = String::new();
    file.take(1024).read_to_string(&mut text).ok()?;
    text.lines().nth(2)?.strip_prefix("pid ")?.parse().ok()
}

#[cfg(not(unix))]
fn owner_pid(_: &Path) -> Option<u32> { None }

#[cfg(not(unix))]
pub(crate) fn address(_: &Path) -> Option<String> { None }

impl Owner {
    pub(crate) fn report_reclaim(&self, verbose: bool) {
        if verbose && let Some(note) = &self.reclaimed { eprintln!("note: {note}"); }
    }

    // @ref LLP 3000.000.000#11-wave-11-factual-release-narration — unlink only our address inode; the ownership sidecar remains named.
    pub(crate) fn clear_address(&self) {
        #[cfg(unix)]
        if let Some(file) = &self.record {
            let path = self.path.with_file_name("serve.lock");
            if let (Ok(held), Ok(named)) = (file.metadata(), fs::symlink_metadata(&path))
                && same_file(&held, &named) {
                let _ = fs::remove_file(path);
            }
        }
    }
    pub(crate) fn worker_lock(&self) -> std::sync::Arc<File> { self._sidecar.clone() }

    pub(crate) fn path(&self) -> Result<&Path, RuntimeError> {
        #[cfg(unix)]
        {
            let meta = snapback4_core::private_fs::database_metadata(&self.path)
                .map_err(|e| RuntimeError::Store(failure(&self.path, e)))?;
            if meta.is_some_and(|m| (m.dev(), m.ino()) == self.identity) { return Ok(&self.path); }
        }
        Err(RuntimeError::Store(failure(&self.path, "owned database moved or was replaced; stop and reopen its current path")))
    }

    pub(crate) fn open_store(&self, timeout: Duration) -> Result<Store, RuntimeError> {
        let mut store = Store::open_with_busy_timeout(self.path()?, timeout)?;
        #[cfg(unix)]
        store.retain_ownership(self._sidecar.clone())?;
        Ok(store)
    }

    pub(crate) fn open_engine(&self, bound: &snapback4_lang::Bound, timeout: Duration, now: i64) -> Result<Engine, RuntimeError> {
        let store = self.open_store(timeout)?;
        if store.meta("release")?.is_some() {
            return Err(RuntimeError::Generation("this store belongs to a deployment; use `deploy` to activate source and `serve` to run it".into()));
        }
        Engine::new_at(store, bound.schema.clone(), bound.programs.iter().map(|p| p.program.clone()), now)
    }
}

impl Drop for Owner { fn drop(&mut self) { self.clear_address(); } }

pub(crate) fn announce(owner: &mut Owner, address: std::net::SocketAddr) -> Result<(), String> {
    // @ref LLP 3000.019#3.4 — live token work runs through the owner, with proof of local store access.
    owner.token_authority = Some(crate::serve::token::publish(owner.path().map_err(|e| e.to_string())?)
        .map_err(|e| failure(&owner.path, e))?);
    let file = owner.record.as_mut().ok_or("device ownership has no server address record")?;
    file.set_len(0).and_then(|_| file.seek(SeekFrom::Start(0))).map_err(|e| e.to_string())?;
    // First-line port is retained for the dev inspector; the third line names the owning process.
    write!(file, "{}\nhttp://{address}\npid {}\n", address.port(), std::process::id()).map_err(|e| e.to_string())
}

